1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,220
The Sun is the most powerful thing in our sky.

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,610
It is also, technically... lazy.

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:09,250
Take one cubic metre from the very centre of its core.

4
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:12,320
It puts out about as much heat as... a compost heap.

5
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:17,150
Your body makes more heat per cubic metre than that. Several times more.

6
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:23,020
Congratulations. You are out-performing a star. Please don't put it on your CV.

7
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:28,940
So how does a compost heap light up the solar system? It's just... very, very big.

8
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:32,450
The light warming your face today was made in the core thousands of years ago.

9
00:00:32,450 --> 00:00:38,750
It spent all that time bumping around inside, then crossed to Earth in eight minutes, like it suddenly remembered it had somewhere to be.

10
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:46,150
Even fusion is a struggle. Protons push each other away. By normal physics, they should basically never touch.

11
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:51,520
They only fuse thanks to quantum tunnelling. Which is physics for: walking through the wall anyway.

12
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:55,780
Even then, the average proton waits about nine billion years for its turn.

13
00:00:55,850 --> 00:01:01,950
Here's the twist. If fusion were easy, the Sun would have burned out long before anything crawled out of the sea.

14
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:07,520
Instead, it took its time. Long enough for oceans... dinosaurs... and you.

15
00:01:07,550 --> 00:01:13,220
So the next time someone calls you lazy, just tell them you're pacing yourself. Like a star.
